; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Spirolaterals
; CATEGORY: Classic Logo
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     patterns, total turning, closing paths, grids
; SUMMARY:  Walk 1, 2, 3 ... N steps turning the same way after each, repeat, and see which spirolaterals come home and which wander away.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   Draws six spirolaterals in a grid. A spirolateral of order N walks
;   1 unit, turns, walks 2 units, turns, and so on up to N units, then
;   starts again at 1. Most of them come back home and close into a
;   pattern; one in the grid never closes, and the label says so.
;   The last one uses a "reversal": on some steps it turns left.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. Try your own with  clean ignore spiro 10 6 90 []  in the
;   command center: unit size, order and angle, then a list of the
;   steps that turn left instead of right.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * nested loops: an inner FOR inside an outer loop
;   * the total turning rule: a path closes when its turning adds up
;     to a whole number of full turns (and it is not a straight line)
;   * why order 4 with 90 degrees runs away
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Find all the orders that never close with 90 degrees. (Try 8, 12.)
;   * Try angle 60 or 120 with orders 3 to 10.
;   * Put a reversal on the first and the last step.
; ============================================================

; Spirolaterals were first described by Frank Odds in the 1970s, and
; teachers soon found they suit the turtle perfectly: each one is
; just FORWARD and RIGHT inside two loops. They fit the idea from
; Turtle Geometry that a path closes when its total turning is a
; multiple of 360.

; One round: walk 1, 2, ... ORDER units. Steps whose numbers are in the
; LEFTS list turn left instead of right. While measuring, every corner
; is added to the box the figure covers.
to spiro.round :unit :order :angle :lefts
  for [i 1 :order] [
    forward :i * :unit
    if :measuring [grow.box]
    ifelse memberp :i :lefts [left :angle] [right :angle]
  ]
end

; Repeat rounds until the turtle is back at the start facing the same
; way, or give up after 12 rounds. Outputs the number of rounds, or 0.
to spiro :unit :order :angle :lefts
  localmake "start pos
  localmake "h heading
  repeat 12 [
    spiro.round :unit :order :angle :lefts
    if and (distance :start) < 0.01 (abs (heading - :h)) < 0.01 [output repcount]
  ]
  output 0
end

to grow.box
  make "box (list min xcor item 1 :box  max xcor item 2 :box  min ycor item 3 :box  max ycor item 4 :box)
end

to min :a :b
  output ifelse :a < :b [:a] [:b]
end

to max :a :b
  output ifelse :a > :b [:a] [:b]
end

; Draw one spirolateral centred in the cell around (X, Y), with a
; caption under it. First a pen-up walk with unit 1 measures it, then
; the unit is chosen so the figure fills a 190 by 150 space.
to cell :x :y :order :angle :lefts :color
  make "measuring "true
  make "box [0 0 0 0]
  penup home
  ignore spiro 1 :order :angle :lefts
  make "measuring "false
  localmake "unit min 190 / ((item 2 :box) - (item 1 :box)) 150 / ((item 4 :box) - (item 3 :box))
  setxy :x - :unit * ((item 1 :box) + (item 2 :box)) / 2 :y + 15 - :unit * ((item 3 :box) + (item 4 :box)) / 2
  setheading 0
  pendown
  setpencolor :color
  localmake "rounds spiro :unit :order :angle :lefts
  penup setxy :x - 70 :y - 95 setheading 0
  setpencolor [60 60 60]
  label (sentence "order :order "angle :angle ifelse emptyp :lefts [[]] [(sentence [left on] :lefts)])
  setxy :x - 70 :y - 113
  ifelse :rounds = 0 [label [never closes]] [label (sentence [closes after] :rounds [rounds])]
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [250 248 242]
setpensize 2
setlabelheight 14
make "measuring "false
cell -265 150 3 90 [] [220 70 60]
cell 0 150 5 90 [] [40 120 210]
cell 265 150 7 90 [] [40 160 80]
cell -265 -130 4 90 [] [150 80 200]
cell 0 -130 5 120 [] [230 140 20]
cell 265 -130 7 90 [3] [200 60 140]
